{"summary":"Verified the current tree. `compute_effective_nitrite_hazard` still has a real IEEE-754 NaN edge case when `chloride_protection_factor == 0.0` and nitrite is tiny positive, and `TankSnapshot` still omits chloride/effective hazard observability. The two blocking amendments are stale: the shrimp pathway already applies chloride-aware nitrite stress, `ShrimpRuntimeParams` already includes `chloride_protection_factor`, trace output already records the required diagnostics, and regression coverage already exists including a salt-treatment scenario.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-3-609a7743","decision":"accept","rationale":"`compute_effective_nitrite_hazard` only guards `nitrite_mg_l <= 0.0`. With tiny positive nitrite, very large chloride, and `chloride_protection_factor == 0.0`, `chloride / nitrite` can overflow to `inf`, `0.0 * inf` becomes `NaN`, and the function returns `NaN`. A fast-path returning raw nitrite when chloride protection is non-positive or chloride is non-positive preserves the intended semantics and avoids the floating-point defect."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-9bf708db","decision":"accept","rationale":"`TankSnapshot` currently exposes nitrite but not chloride concentration or effective nitrite hazard, so callers using snapshots cannot monitor salt-treatment state except indirectly through events or traces. That is a real observability gap for this feature. Adding at least `chloride_mg_per_l`, and ideally effective hazard as well, would improve API/TUI usefulness without changing core behavior."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-685b9673","decision":"reject","rationale":"The amendment is factually wrong against the current code. `step_hourly_shrimp_stress` and `update_condition` both call `compute_nitrite_stress_diagnostics`, which derives stress from chloride-adjusted effective hazard rather than raw nitrite. `ShrimpRuntimeParams` already includes `chloride_protection_factor`. As implemented, tanks with equal NO2 and different chloride do not behave identically."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-4a3fdfe3","decision":"reject","rationale":"The claimed coverage and diagnostics gaps are already addressed in the current tree. There are dedicated chloride/nitrite tests, including monotonic hazard reduction, species-parameterized protection, stress/mortality differences under different chloride levels, and an end-to-end salt-treatment emergency scenario. The engine trace for `system:shrimp_stress` already records nitrite, chloride, effective hazard, stress increment, and accumulator values."}]}